 What is ARM?
ARM (Azure Resource Manager) is the deployment system used to:
Create
Update
Delete Azure resources

It works via:
ARM templates (JSON)
Terraform (uses ARM APIs under the hood)

🔄 What is an “ARM Upgrade”?
An ARM upgrade means making changes to existing Azure infrastructure managed through ARM

🔧 Examples of ARM Upgrades
✅ 1. Resource Configuration Upgrade

Increase VM size:
Standard_DS2 → Standard_DS3
Change App Service Plan SKU:
S1 → P1v3
Upgrade Storage performance tier:
Standard → Premium

✅ 2. Security / Compliance Upgrade

Enable private endpoints
Restrict public access
Upgrade TLS version (e.g., TLS 1.0 → 1.2)


✅ 3. Platform / Version Upgrade

AKS Kubernetes version upgrade
App Service runtime version change
OS image upgrade in VM


✅ 4. Networking Changes

Modify NSG rules
Add load balancer
Update subnet configuration


✅ 5. Provider / API Upgrade (Terraform Side)

Upgrade Terraform provider:

terraform init -upgrade

Moves to newer ARM API versions internally


🧠 How ARM Upgrade Happens (Behind the scenes)
When you run:
terraform apply
👉 Terraform:
Compares current state vs desired state
Calls Azure ARM APIs
Applies changes incrementally


🔁 Types of ARM Upgrades
✅ In-place Update (Safe)
Resource stays same, only config changes
Example:
Increase storage quota

⚠️ Recreate (Risky Upgrade)
Resource is destroyed and recreated

Example:
Changing storage account replication type (sometimes)
Changing resource name

⚠️ Risk:
Data loss
Downtime


✅ Rolling Upgrade
Used for:
VMSS
AKS nodes
Gradually replaces instances

✅ Blue-Green Upgrade
New infra created
Traffic switched later

Used in enterprise production

🔍 Why ARM Upgrades Are Important
Improve performance
Enhance security
Adopt latest Azure features
Meet compliance requirements
Fix deprecated configurations


⚠️ Key Risks in ARM Upgrades
Accidental resource deletion
owntime
Data loss (especially storage, DB)
Dependency break (apps losing connection)
Downtime
Data loss (especially storage, DB)
Dependency break (apps losing connection)


##########Quick Cheat Sheet: Azure Resources (What Causes Recreate)###########################

👉 Rule: If Terraform shows “forces replacement”, the resource will be destroyed & recreated.
🔴 HIGH-RISK (Avoid Recreate – Data Loss Possible)
🗄️ Storage Account
Forces recreate if you change:
name
location
account_kind (StorageV2 → BlobStorage)
Some replication changes (LRS ↔ GRS in certain cases)

✅ Safe changes:
Tags
Network rules
TLS version


🛢️ Azure SQL / Databases
Forces recreate:
Server name
Collation
Region

✅ Safe:
Scaling (DTU/vCore)
Backup retention

🔐 Key Vault
Forces recreate:
Name
Region
Soft-delete settings (older configs)

✅ Safe:
Access policies



🟡 MEDIUM RISK
💻 Virtual Machines
May recreate if:
OS disk change
Image change

✅ In-place:
VM size (sometimes restart required)


⚙️ App Service
Forces recreate:
Name (hostname tied)

✅ In-place:
Scaling plan
App settings


🟢 LOW RISK (Rolling Supported)
☸️ AKS Cluster
✅ Uses rolling upgrade:

Node pool upgrade
Kubernetes version

❌ Recreate if:
Cluster name
Region


📦 VM Scale Set (VMSS)
✅ Rolling update:
Image upgrade
Instance update

🌐 Load Balancer / Networking
✅ Mostly in-place:

NSG rules
Backend pool updates

⚠️ Recreate if:
Changing subnet CIDR
Major structural changes


💥 2. Real Failure Scenario (VERY IMPORTANT – Interview Gold)
🔴 Scenario: Storage Account Got Deleted in Production
🎯 Situation:
Engineer changed this in Terraform:
Terraformname = "newstorageprod123"Show more lines
🔎 What Terraform Plan Showed:
-/+ azurerm_storage_account.prod
(forces replacement)

❌ Mistake:

Engineer ignored plan
Ran:

Shellterraform applyShow more lines

💣 What Happened:

Old storage account deleted
New storage created
✅ Infra succeeded…
❌ ALL DATA LOST
❌ Applications failed (no blobs/files)


🔥 Impact:

Web apps crashed
Data permanently lost (no backup)
Sev-1 incident


✅ Root Cause:

Didn’t review terraform plan
No backup strategy
No blue-green approach


✅ Correct Approach (What SHOULD be done):

Create new storage account
Copy data:

Shellazcopy copy ...Show more lines

Update app connection string
Test application
Remove old storage later

👉 This is called Blue-Green Strategy


